    "case_id": "amb2-case03-agency-acquisition",
    "focal_region": {
      "annotated_sentence_text": "[Turn 1 USER]\ne001\tMy cofounder and I run a twelve-person research agency, and a larger consultancy has offered to acquire us.\ne002\tThe price would give both of us meaningful security, and the buyer says our team would remain intact as a specialist unit.\ne003\tMy cofounder sees the offer as recognition of what we built and is ready to move quickly.\ne004\tI am attracted to the security but worried that our methods will become a sales story while project decisions move elsewhere.\ne005\tI cannot tell whether my hesitation protects the work or protects my identity as an independent founder.\n[Turn 1 ASSISTANT]\ne006\tThe offer combines liquidity, team continuity, method ownership, and your future role.\ne007\tThose interests can move together in the buyer's narrative while diverging after closing.\ne008\tAsk which commitments can be contractual, which depend on the acquiring leader, and which are inherently temporary.\ne009\tYour identity concern may be real without invalidating the operating concern, so do not force one explanation to displace the other.\n[Turn 7 USER]\ne050\tI am leaning toward signing a nonbinding term sheet that resolves the staffing floor, project-refusal boundary, earn-out mechanics, and employee consultation before any exclusivity period begins.\ne051\tI will tell the buyer I intend to evaluate the two-year leadership role in good faith, but I am not willing to commit to staying until I meet the executive and understand who decides project framing.\ne052\tI am also prepared to end the talks if the global-client exception makes the refusal right meaningless.\ne053\tI have not resolved how much employee uncertainty is justified before we know whether those protections survive detailed drafting.\n[Turn 7 ASSISTANT]\ne054\tThe position separates willingness to document acquisition terms from commitment to the two-year role and from acceptance of exclusivity.\ne055\tThe reporting relationship and meaningful refusal right remain conditions, while employee consultation remains a timing problem rather than a solved protection.\ne056\tA term sheet can expose whether the buyer will make its promises concrete.\ne057\tIt cannot prevent momentum from narrowing your perceived alternatives or remove the cost of involving employees before a deal is certain.",
